      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To determine pain assessment and management, and the use of analgesics in dermatology."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Two hundred and sixty six patients hospitalised in a dermatology university department (Henri-Mondor, Créteil) between November 1999 and April 2000 were enrolled in a prospective study. Clinical evaluation of pain intensity and evolution were studied using a visual analogic scale (VAS) pain score at presentation, during hospitalisation, and at discharge. Prescription and consumption of analgesics were also studied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Fifty-nine percent of the patients experienced pain. Eighty-four percent of them had mild or moderate to severe pain (VAS<7/10) and were relieved with non or mild opioid analgesics (discharge VAS<4/10). Sixteen percent of them had intense pain requiring morphine (VAS score >=7/10)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Pain management is very important in dermatology. Every physician should know its principles since dermatologists play a crucial role in the patient's care."
        }
      ]},
